-- title:
--   Specialisation homomorphism for a family over ℚ[X]
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over the polynomial ring $\mathbb{Q}[X]$ and let $t_0 \in \mathbb{Q}$ be such that the discriminant $W.\Delta \in \mathbb{Q}[X]$ does not vanish at $t_0$. Write $K_0 = \mathrm{Frac}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}[X])$, the field generated by $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}[X]$. The assertion is that there exists an additive group homomorphism $s$ from the group of points of the base change of $W$ to $K_0$ to the group of points of the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the specialised curve $W$ mapped along $\mathrm{eval}_{t_0} : \mathbb{Q}[X] \to \mathbb{Q}$, with three properties. First, $s$ is equivariant for the action of $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphisms $\sigma$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$: for every $\sigma$ and every point $P$ over $K_0$, $s(\mathrm{galLift}(\sigma) \cdot P) = \sigma \cdot s(P)$, where $\mathrm{galLift}(\sigma)$ is the $\mathbb{Q}[X]$-algebra automorphism of $K_0$ obtained by extending the coefficientwise action of $\sigma$ on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}[X]$ to its fraction field. Second, $s$ is injective on torsion in the strong form: for every $n \neq 0$ and every $P$ with $n \cdot P = 0$, $s(P) = 0$ implies $P = 0$. Third, $s$ is given by evaluation at $t_0$ on points with polynomial coordinates: for $x, y \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}[X]$ whose images in $K_0$ form a nonsingular affine point of the base-changed curve, $s$ of that affine point equals $\mathrm{pt}$ of the base change of the specialised curve at $(x(t_0), y(t_0))$, the images of $t_0$ being taken in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; here $\mathrm{pt}\,W\,x\,y$ denotes the affine point $(x,y)$ when it is nonsingular and $0$ otherwise.
--
--   This is the specialisation (reduction) homomorphism for a one-parameter family of Weierstrass curves at a rational parameter of good reduction, realised as reduction at the discrete valuation ring $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}[X]_{(X - t_0)}$, whose residue field is $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; since the residue characteristic is $0$, injectivity holds on the full torsion subgroup. It is used in the construction of the Rubin–Silverberg family, being cited by [`RubinSilverberg.exists_torsionBy_linearEquiv_rsMember`](thm.html#RubinSilverberg.exists_torsionBy_linearEquiv_rsMember) to transport torsion Galois modules from the generic fibre to a specialised curve.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_WeierstrassCurve_exists_specializationHom.lean

import Definitions.Def_EllipticCurve_RubinSilverbergFamily
